" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097572 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097573 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097573 #edb/ref 1061 19724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097573 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "003b2c9e-f558-5f16-ae29-70cb592bdea5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097573 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097574 #edb/ref 1061 19725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097574 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "82273a54-9ebb-549c-bc34-a98726936b3c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097574 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097633 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097633 #edb/ref 1061 23091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097633 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3eccc6e2-615b-5126-b1a2-9f9bb514c40d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097633 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097855 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to know what are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola below.\n\n![](images/5d/5d3493115eb11315849d3d7c247b9f63a399c6ab7ba2c226b0b84958a47d3a21.png)\n\nThen, we proceed as follows: \n\nFirst, we find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has its vertex at $\\displaystyle\\left(\\frac 32, -1\\right)$ and passes through the point $\\displaystyle\\left(1,-\\frac 34\\right).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^2 = 4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k) = \\left(\\dfrac 32, -1\\right)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =4p(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $\\displaystyle\\left(1,-\\frac 34\\right),$ we substitute $x= 1$ and $y=-\\dfrac34$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =4p(-\\frac{3}{4}+1) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =4p(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y= 0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =(0+1) \\\\ {(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x-\\frac{3}{2} & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are $\\dfrac12$ and $\\dfrac52.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097855 #edb/ref 1065 3484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097855 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "04c720cc-0a5d-5a6c-acf5-65c378ef274b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097855 #edb/ref 1067 "Calculating the Intercepts of a Parabola Given a Graph of the Parabola" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097868 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider the parabola $(x-1)^2 = \\dfrac 1 2 (y+3) ,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/bb/bbe360c9580cab435e5b737c80c5381f039d1871250cdae3c16bc686e3d0a53d.png)\n\n  \n\nTo calculate the $y$-intercept of the parabola (shown as the point $A$ in the diagram), we set $x=0$ and solve for $y.$ In our case, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(y+3) \\\\ (0-1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(y+3) \\\\ (-1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(y+3) \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{1}{2}(y+3) \\\\ 2 & =y+3 \\\\ y & =2-3 \\\\ y & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the coordinates of the $y$-intercept are $(0,-1).$\n\nOn the other hand, to find the $x$-intercepts (shown as the points $B$ and $C$ in the diagram), we set $y=0$ and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(y+3) \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(0+3) \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coordinates of $B$ are $\\left(1-\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3}{2}},0\\right)$ and the coordinates of $C$ are $\\left(1+\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3}{2}},0\\right).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097868 #edb/ref 1065 3905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097868 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f9511803-ede3-5b03-b34f-87d1bb050901" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097868 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281802 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\sqrt{20}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281802 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281802 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0c70e801-fd99-586e-ad50-fca199a9d991" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283022 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\sqrt{15}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283022 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283022 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "99578261-0e54-5ddf-9cbe-2131732d1331" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283244 #edb/ref 1080 "-10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283244 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283244 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b33f1a06-1ff7-5054-9b68-11c4a5d9ab9b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283463 #edb/ref 1080 "-9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283463 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283463 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cacc8365-ba97-5758-a9a1-2e6465cbb4d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283903 #edb/ref 1080 "-12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283903 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283903 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fca7c681-e646-5b9c-9404-16ca07e77a34" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284341 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186281802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284341 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186283022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284341 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186283244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284341 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186283463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284341 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186283903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284341 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284341 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284341 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186283244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284341 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e67bd542-ad74-5a89-8312-e4d33f763205" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284548 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284548 #edb/ref 1069 "q-165433" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284548 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284548 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/61/616116b9e5cf137c526b11dc5ce1647cd8f9cbd3a652be6dade9a8769467c27c.png)\n\nWhat is the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284548 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a86ebeb5-18d1-5f84-a46f-2e3c446ad259" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284548 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(5,15)$ and passes through the point $(2,6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(5,15)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(x-5)^{2}=4p(y-15).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(2,6),$ we substitute $x=2$ and $y=6$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2-5)^{2} & =4p(6-15) \\\\ 9 & =4p(-9) \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-5)^{2} & =-(y-15).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-5)^{2} & =-(y-15) \\\\ 25 & =-y+15 \\\\ y & =15-25 \\\\ y & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $-10.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186289184 #edb/ref 1080 "-8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186289184 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186289184 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "adec1f59-4414-5eba-a9d0-0be0539bcf76" #