50c0b2bbc1d3c6323b09b9d6dc5272f67a505b80f0688b16ae.png)\n\nThe value of the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above  is{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186292535 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c2c4d5ba-f8b4-53aa-9498-734e5ac94d27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186292535 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-3,-2)$ and passes through the point $(-7,6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(-3,-2)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(x+3)^{2}=4p(y+2).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-7,6),$ we substitute $x=-7$ and $y=6$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-7+3)^{2} & =4p(6+2) \\\\ 16 & =4p(8) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =2(y+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0+3)^{2} & =2(y+2) \\\\ 9 & =2(y+2) \\\\ \\frac{9}{2} & =y+2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{9}{2}-2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $\\frac{5}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346404 #edb/ref 1080 "$1$ and $-3$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346404 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346404 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "781aff63-be25-5dd9-a51d-fd41b763b3cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346521 #edb/ref 1080 "$\\frac{3}{2}$ and $-\\frac{5}{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346521 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346521 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "86da7198-114b-5a34-b00b-6bfb9d90ae08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346556 #edb/ref 1080 "$3$ and $-5$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346556 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346556 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8b445de7-b798-5c9d-80fe-4e38cff789ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346637 #edb/ref 1080 "$\\frac{5}{2}$ and $-\\frac{7}{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346637 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346637 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "92de7067-16c8-564e-b4a0-1c6307b5d500" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347272 #edb/ref 1080 "$2$ and $-4$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347272 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347272 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "da9e8b39-ca80-5e20-a05d-52cf6b3dd6f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347621 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186346404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347621 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186346521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347621 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186346556 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347621 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186346637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347621 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186347272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347621 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347621 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347621 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186346404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347621 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "06b9448a-a274-5dad-828f-e13b7bb51e5b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348183 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348183 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79252" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348183 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348183 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/aa/aa95f5a4629b2f97943db55125f91d76de962534be820fe70112132e5fddaaec.png)\n\nWhat are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348183 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a3a199da-f774-5b31-869b-a8666e40a01a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348183 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-1,-4)$ and passes through the point $(2,5).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-1,-4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =4p(y+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(2,5),$ we substitute $x=2$ and $y=5$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2+1)^{2} & =4p(5+4) \\\\ 9 & =4p(9) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =y+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =0+4 \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{4} \\\\ x+1 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =1,-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are $1$ and $-3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403703 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186403704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403703 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73832" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403703 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403703 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e7/e79a3a7c2fce5a3e3ec7d838ebe1327dcc5452168f499fad05eea85a08e8f8b0.png)\n\nWhat are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403703 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "166cdda3-01a4-5828-9800-429450a46515" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403703 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(2,-1)$ and passes through the point $(-2,3).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(2,-1)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-2,3),$ we substitute $x=-2$ and $y=3$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2-2)^{2} & =4p(3+1) \\\\ 16 & =4p(4) \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4(0+1) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{4} \\\\ x & =\\pm 2+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are $0$ and $4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403704 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403704 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403704 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403704 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403704 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403704 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403704 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403704 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186403708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403704 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "199aaf87-46d2-5438-b5f3-ee557ec29887" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403705 #edb/ref 1080 "$0$  and $\\sqrt{5}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403705 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403705 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "544b7ae8-40b2-5ec8-83d6-d19730e6b5a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403706 #edb/ref 1080 "$0$ and $5$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403706 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403706 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2316f0bc-0936-5acf-bd1b-fe6117ca3155" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403707 #edb/ref 1080 "$0$ and $3$" #edb/ref 175921